  </a>
  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2510.01237v1 [cs.CL] 23 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Confidence-Aware Routing for Large Language Model Reliability Enhancement: A Multi-Signal Approach to Pre-Generation Hallucination Mitigation</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Nandakishor M
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI Safety Research</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Convai Innovations</span>
<br class="ltx_break">nandakishor@convaiinnovations.com

</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models suffer from hallucination, generating plausible yet factually incorrect content. Current mitigation strategies focus on post-generation correction, which is computationally expensive and fails to prevent unreliable content generation. We propose a confidence-aware routing system that proactively assesses model uncertainty before generation and redirects queries based on estimated reliability. Our approach combines three complementary signals: semantic alignment between internal representations and reference embeddings, internal convergence analysis across model layers, and learned confidence estimation. The unified confidence score determines routing to four pathways: local generation for high confidence, retrieval-augmented generation for medium confidence, larger models for low confidence, and human review for very low confidence. Evaluation on knowledge-intensive QA benchmarks demonstrates significant improvements in hallucination detection (0.74 vs. 0.42 baseline) while reducing computational costs by 40% compared to post-hoc methods. The F1 score improves from 0.61 to 0.82 with low false positive rates (0.09). This paradigm shift from reactive correction to proactive assessment offers a computationally efficient approach to LLM reliability enhancement.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Index Terms: </h6>Hallucination Detection, Large Language Models, Confidence Estimation, Model Reliability, Uncertainty Quantification, Routing Systems, Pre-Generation Mitigation

</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large Language Models have demonstrated transformative capabilities in natural language understanding and generation, revolutionizing applications from conversational AI to knowledge extraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. However, a persistent challenge limiting their deployment in critical applications is hallucination, where models generate fluent yet factually incorrect or unsupported content <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. These fabricated responses can appear highly convincing, making them particularly dangerous in high-stakes domains such as medical diagnosis, legal advice, or scientific research.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Existing approaches to hallucination mitigation largely focus on post-generation correction. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems ground responses in external knowledge bases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, while consistency-based methods detect hallucinations by comparing multiple model outputs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. Although effective to varying degrees, these approaches share a fundamental limitation: they address symptoms rather than causes, operating after potentially unreliable content has been generated.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a paradigm shift from post-generation correction to pre-generation assessment. Our confidence-aware routing system evaluates query-specific model reliability before generation begins, enabling proactive redirection of uncertain queries to more appropriate response mechanisms. This approach offers several advantages: it prevents the generation of unreliable content entirely, reduces computational waste on likely-to-fail queries, and provides interpretable confidence estimates for downstream decision-making.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions include: (1) A multi-signal confidence estimation framework combining semantic alignment, internal convergence, and learned uncertainty measures, (2) A deterministic routing system that maps confidence scores to appropriate response pathways, (3) Comprehensive empirical evaluation demonstrating effectiveness across knowledge-intensive benchmarks, and (4) Analysis of computational efficiency gains compared to post-hoc correction approaches.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hallucination Detection and Mitigation</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hallucination detection methods can be broadly categorized into training-time and inference-time approaches. Training-time methods include improved data curation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, factual knowledge editing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, and specialized loss functions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Inference-time approaches encompass retrieval augmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, consistency checking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, and uncertainty estimation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has explored internal model states for hallucination detection. Farquhar et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite> develop entropy-based uncertainty estimators using hidden representations. Chen et al. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite> propose discriminators trained on internal activations. Our approach builds on these foundations but focuses specifically on pre-generation assessment rather than post-hoc detection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Uncertainty Quantification in Neural Networks</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Uncertainty quantification in deep learning distinguishes between epistemic uncertainty (model uncertainty) and aleatoric uncertainty (data uncertainty) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. For language models, approaches include Bayesian neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, ensemble methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, and dropout-based approximations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Recent work extends these concepts to large-scale language models through sampling-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> and probe-based techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mixture of Experts and Routing</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures use learned routing to direct inputs to specialized sub-networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. While traditional MoE routing optimizes for task performance, our confidence-aware routing prioritizes reliability assessment. Recent work explores LLM-based routing for improved expert selection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, providing conceptual foundations for our approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Methodology</span></h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Problem Formulation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> be a language model, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> a query, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=M(Q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=M(Q)</annotation></semantics></math> the generated response. Traditional approaches assess <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(R\text{ is hallucinated}|R,Q,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> is hallucinated</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(R\text{ is hallucinated}|R,Q,M)</annotation></semantics></math> after generation. We instead estimate <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(M\text{ will hallucinate}|Q,M)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> will hallucinate</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(M\text{ will hallucinate}|Q,M)</annotation></semantics></math> before generation, enabling proactive routing decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, we define a confidence estimator <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C:\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C:\mathcal{Q}\rightarrow[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> that maps queries to confidence scores, and a routing function <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A:[0,1]\rightarrow\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A:[0,1]\rightarrow\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> that maps confidence scores to actions, where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{\text{local},\text{rag},\text{large},\text{human}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mtext>local</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>rag</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>large</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext>human</mtext></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{\text{local},\text{rag},\text{large},\text{human}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Signal Confidence Estimation</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our confidence estimation combines three complementary signals:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semantic Alignment (<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{sem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">sem</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{sem}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> We measure alignment between the model’s internal representation and a reference embedding. Given query <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract the model’s final hidden state <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}</annotation></semantics></math> and compare it with a reference embedding <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{e}_{\text{ref}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{e}_{\text{ref}}</annotation></semantics></math> from a reliable embedding model:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{sem}}=\cos(\mathbf{P}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}),\mathbf{e}_{\text{ref}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>sem</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐏</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{sem}}=\cos(\mathbf{P}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}),\mathbf{e}_{\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is a learned projection network trained to map internal representations to the reference space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Internal Convergence (<math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{conv}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">conv</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{conv}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> We analyze the stability of processing across model layers. For a sequence of hidden states <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{h}_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{h}_{l}\}_{l=1}^{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute variance reduction as an indicator of convergent processing:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="C_{\text{conv}}=\frac{\text{Var}(\mathbf{h}_{1:L/2})}{\text{Var}(\mathbf{h}_{L/2:L})+\epsilon}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>conv</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐡</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{conv}}=\frac{\text{Var}(\mathbf{h}_{1:L/2})}{\text{Var}(\mathbf{h}_{L/2:L})+\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Learned Confidence (<math id="S3.SS2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{learned}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">learned</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{learned}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span> We train a neural network <math id="S3.SS2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> to predict confidence directly from internal activations:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{learned}}=\phi(\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>learned</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{learned}}=\phi(\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The overall confidence score combines these signals:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{\text{overall}}=w_{1}C_{\text{sem}}+w_{2}C_{\text{conv}}+w_{3}C_{\text{learned}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>overall</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>sem</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>conv</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>learned</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{\text{overall}}=w_{1}C_{\text{sem}}+w_{2}C_{\text{conv}}+w_{3}C_{\text{learned}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">where weights <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are learned through validation on labeled data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Routing Function</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the confidence score, we implement deterministic routing:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A(C_{\text{overall}})=\begin{cases}\text{local}&amp;\text{if }C_{\text{overall}}\geq\theta_{\text{high}}\\
\text{rag}&amp;\text{if }\theta_{\text{med}}\leq C_{\text{overall}}&lt;\theta_{\text{high}}\\
\text{large}&amp;\text{if }\theta_{\text{low}}\leq C_{\text{overall}}&lt;\theta_{\text{med}}\\
\text{human}&amp;\text{if }C_{\text{overall}}&lt;\theta_{\text{low}}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>overall</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>local</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>overall</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>rag</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>med</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>overall</mtext></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>large</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>overall</mtext></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>med</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>human</mtext></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mtext>overall</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(C_{\text{overall}})=\begin{cases}\text{local}&amp;\text{if }C_{\text{overall}}\geq\theta_{\text{high}}\\
\text{rag}&amp;\text{if }\theta_{\text{med}}\leq C_{\text{overall}}&lt;\theta_{\text{high}}\\
\text{large}&amp;\text{if }\theta_{\text{low}}\leq C_{\text{overall}}&lt;\theta_{\text{med}}\\
\text{human}&amp;\text{if }C_{\text{overall}}&lt;\theta_{\text{low}}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Thresholds <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{high}},\theta_{\text{med}},\theta_{\text{low}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>med</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{high}},\theta_{\text{med}},\theta_{\text{low}}</annotation></semantics></math> are determined through validation to optimize the trade-off between accuracy and computational cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Setup</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Datasets and Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate on knowledge-intensive QA benchmarks including Natural Questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, TriviaQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, and HotpotQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>. We use both existing datasets with ground-truth labels and construct synthetic evaluation sets by systematically introducing factual errors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Primary metrics include:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hallucination Detection Rate:</span> Fraction of hallucinations correctly identified</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">False Positive Rate:</span> Fraction of correct responses incorrectly flagged</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Routing Accuracy:</span> Alignment between routing decisions and optimal actions</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Computational Efficiency:</span> Total inference cost compared to baseline approaches</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Model Configuration</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We implement our approach using HuggingFace’s SmolLM2-360M-Instruct as the primary language model, a compact 360-million parameter instruction-tuned model designed for efficient inference. For reference embeddings, we employ Sentence-BERT (all-MiniLM-L6-v2) which provides 384-dimensional normalized embeddings. The projection network <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐏</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a deep architecture with layer normalization, dropout regularization, and residual connections to prevent overfitting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The confidence predictor <math id="S4.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> implements a multi-layer perceptual network with progressive dimensionality reduction: from the model’s hidden size to half-size through a 4-layer architecture with batch normalization and dropout. We use AdamW optimizer with learning rate 2e-4, weight decay 1e-4, and learning rate scheduling with plateau-based reduction for stable training convergence.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Training Procedure</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Training proceeds in two phases using a carefully curated dataset designed to capture diverse confidence scenarios:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Preparation:</span> We construct a balanced training set of 72 examples categorized into three confidence levels: 33 high-confidence examples covering factual knowledge and technical concepts, 27 low-confidence examples including personal information and temporal queries, and 12 medium-confidence examples representing subjective or opinion-based queries.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Projection Model Training:</span> We train the confidence estimation components over 30 epochs using a combined loss function that incorporates semantic alignment loss, direct confidence supervision with mean squared error, and L2 regularization. The training achieves convergence with final total loss of 0.1633, demonstrating effective learning of confidence patterns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Threshold Calibration:</span> Routing thresholds are empirically set at <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{high}}=0.75" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>high</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.75</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{high}}=0.75</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{med}}=0.55" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>med</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.55</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{med}}=0.55</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{\text{low}}=0.35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mtext>low</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{\text{low}}=0.35</annotation></semantics></math> based on validation performance to optimize the trade-off between accuracy and computational efficiency.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Results and Analysis</span></h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overall Performance</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ V-A Overall Performance ‣ V Results and Analysis ‣ Confidence-Aware Routing for Large Language Model Reliability Enhancement: A Multi-Signal Approach to Pre-Generation Hallucination Mitigation" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> presents our main experimental results. The confidence-aware routing system achieves substantial improvements in hallucination detection while maintaining high accuracy on correctly answered queries.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">TABLE I: </span>Main Results on Knowledge-Intensive QA Benchmarks</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T1.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Halluc. Det.</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">False Pos.</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F1</span></th>
<th id="S5.T1.2.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cost</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S5.T1.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Baseline</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.42</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.15</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0.61</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">1.0x</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">SelfCheckGPT</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.68</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.12</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.76</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.3.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">4.2x</td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T1.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S5.T1.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">RAG (Always)</th>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.71</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.08</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.80</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.4.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">2.8x</td></tr>
